-- title:
--   Indexing a large enough set by the cyclic group of order $a$
-- statement:
--   Let $\alpha$ be a type, let $a$ be a nonzero natural number, and let $S \subseteq \alpha$ satisfy
--
--   $$a \;\le\; \#S .$$
--
--   Then $S$ contains $a$ distinct, cyclically indexed representatives: there is an injective map
--
--   $$\rho : \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z} \longrightarrow \alpha \qquad\text{with}\qquad \rho(i) \in S \ \text{ for every } i \in \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}.$$
--
--   In other words, an injection $\mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow S$ exists as soon as $S$ has at least $a$ elements.
--
--   The result is what converts a size hypothesis on a set into a concrete indexing of it by a cyclic group, so that constructions phrased in terms of indices read modulo $a$ — for instance a scheme in which consecutive positions carry indices $i$ and $i+j$ — can be run inside that set.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The cardinality is `Set.ncard`, which is $0$ on infinite sets; combined with $a \ne 0$ (the `NeZero a` instance) the hypothesis $a \le \#S$ therefore also forces $S$ to be *finite*, with at least $a$ elements. The map $\rho$ is total on $\mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$ and lands in $S$, but is typed into $\alpha$ rather than into the subtype.
